dir/index.yo
description: The same overlay contract, with the instance overlay as a plain index.yo file
- >
  A dir carrying an `index.yo` file - the SAME overlay contract as
  [dir/.yo](::language:concretes:03-yamlover:01-dir:00-dir_yo), with the instance overlay moved
  out of the control subdirectory and into one plain file inside the directory it controls:
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  julia/
    index.yo   - the INSTANCE overlay (what .yo/body.yo holds in the other flavor)
    name       - "Julia"
    age        - 42
- >
  Everything `body.yo` can say, `index.yo` says: the scalar self-value, the overlay-only keys, the
  pointers and anchors, and the positional pointer-array that grants ORDER. Only the LOCATION
  differs, so a directory converts between the two flavors by moving ONE file.
- >
  ONE file is all that moves. `.yo/` is untouched by the choice - it keeps `meta.yo`, the index
  cache, the sidecars and the trash, read and written exactly as
  [the other flavor](::language:concretes:03-yamlover:01-dir:00-dir_yo) does, and a `dir/index.yo`
  grows one the moment it needs any of them. What changes is only that such a `.yo/` has no body
  of its own to hold, which is no longer the debris the layout invariants look for.
- >
  The overlay file is not an entry. `index.yo` is CONSUMED by the directory the way `.yo/body.yo`
  is - it is never rendered as a key, which is this concrete's one limitation: a `dir/index.yo`
  cannot hold a member NAMED `index.yo`. Nor can it hold both overlays: `index.yo` beside a
  `.yo/body.yo` is a
  [layout violation](::language:concretes:02-invariants), the duplicate-overlay diagnostic.
